window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"21-06","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"21-06-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-06-01/","title_he":"א – מצוות המזוזה","title_en":"1. The Mitzva of Mezuza","body_en":"<p>The Torah commanded every person of Israel to affix a mezuza on the doorways of his house and its rooms. The mezuza is a parchment on which are written the two passages that we were commanded to write on the doorposts of the house: the passage of 'Shema' (Devarim 6:4-9) and the passage of 'Ve-haya im shamo'a' (there 11:13-21), in which it is said: \"And you shall write them on the doorposts of your house and on your gates\". We say these passages also within the mitzva of 'the recitation of the Shema'. And the tefillin too, which contain four passages, include these two passages.</p>\n<p>In general, in the passage of 'Shema' we take upon ourselves the yoke of the Kingdom of Heaven, and in the passage of 'Ve-haya im shamo'a' the yoke of the mitzvot (Berakhot 14b; see Peninei Halakha, Prayer 15:1; 3-4). In more detail, in the passage of 'Shema' are stated the foundations of the faith in God's unity, which was revealed to the world through the people of Israel, as it is said: \"Hear, O Israel, the Lord is our God, the Lord is One\". From the recognition that God is One it is understood that He gives life to everything, and all values receive their meaning from cleaving to Him; therefore \"And you shall love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your might\". So that faith will accompany our lives, we were commanded that the words of the Torah be upon our hearts, that we teach them to our children, and that we engage in them when we sit at home and when we walk on the way, when we lie down and when we rise.</p>\n<p>In the passage of 'Ve-haya im shamo'a' the foundation of Divine providence is explained, according to which, if we listen to the voice of God, love Him and keep His mitzvot, we will merit the Divine blessing – the rain will fall in its season and the land will give its produce. But if we serve other gods, or become enslaved to evil inclinations, or bow down to foreign ideologies, which are also idols – God will stop granting us His blessing, we will perish from the good land that He gave us and go into exile. But the punishment too, like the mitzvot, is for our good, so that we will return in repentance, as it is said at the end of the passage: \"So that your days may be multiplied, and the days of your children, upon the land which the Lord swore to your fathers to give them, as the days of the heavens above the earth\".</p>\n<p>So that these ideas will always accompany us as we come into the house and as we go out of it, we were commanded to affix them on the doorposts of our doorways. The meaning of the word 'mezuzot' in the Torah is the vertical posts standing on both sides of the doorway, on which the lintel rests. With time the name 'mezuza' passed to the parchment on which the passages of 'Shema' and 'Ve-haya im shamo'a' are written.</p>\n<p>The mitzva is to write the passages of the mezuza in ink on parchment in STaM script for the sake of the sanctity of mezuza (below 10:4; 6-10; 16). The two passages are written on one parchment and in one column (Shulḥan Arukh 288:1-2). The space made between the two passages is that of a closed passage, and if he made it as an open passage – it is valid (Shulḥan Arukh 288:13; above 4:9).</p>\n<p>The mezuza is rolled from the end of the line to its beginning (Shulḥan Arukh 288:14), it is inserted into a 'mezuza case', and it is affixed to the doorpost of the doorway so that the first line faces upwards (below halakha 4). Before affixing the mezuza the blessing is recited (below halakha 3). The mezuza is affixed on the right side of one entering (below 7), within the space of the doorway (below 8), and in the upper third (below 6). It is a mitzva to affix a mezuza also on the gates of courtyards and of the cities of Israel, as it is said \"and on your gates\" (below 7:6).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"21-06-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-06-02/","title_he":"ב – סגולת השמירה","title_en":"2. The Special Protection","body_en":"<p>There is a special virtue in the mitzva of mezuza, in that it grants protection and long life to the householders and their children, as it is said at the end of the passage of 'Ve-haya im shamo'a' (there 11:21): \"So that your days may be multiplied, and the days of your children, upon the land which the Lord swore to your fathers to give them, as the days of the heavens above the earth\". And the Sages even learned from here that for the sin of neglecting the mitzva of mezuza children may die (Shabbat 32b; Shulḥan Arukh, Yoreh De'ah 285:1).</p>\n<p>The house protects a person from rain and wind, sun and wild beasts, thieves and robbers. However, however strong the house may be, the dangers lying in wait for a person are many, and the main danger standing at his door is that, out of the illusion of stability that the house gives, a person will forget the Lord his God, who breathed a soul into him and commanded him to walk in His ways and to bring goodness and blessing to the world.</p>\n<p>Therefore we were commanded to affix the mezuza at the doorway of the house, so that every time a person enters his house or leaves it, he will remember the foundations of faith written in it. And through this he will remember his purpose and the value of his existence, and will fulfil the mitzvot of the Torah, and God will strengthen his life, and blessing will rest on his home and on his work, and dangers and temptations will keep far from him and will not shorten his days and the days of his children. So, by the merit of the mezuza, his house will be a faithful house in Israel, and God will lengthen his days and the days of his children for all generations (see Zohar 3, Ra'aya Mehemna 265b).</p>\n<p>In order to remember the mezuza and the ideas in it, many are accustomed to place their hand on the mezuza when they enter the house and when they leave it (Rema, Yoreh De'ah 285:2). And some even kiss their hand afterwards (Birkei Yosef). However, a sick person should refrain from touching it, so as not to infect people with his illness.</p>\n<p>The Sages said (Shabbat 23b): \"One who is careful with mezuza merits a beautiful dwelling.\" The main beauty of the dwelling is that it expresses, through the mezuza, the good aspirations of the owner of the dwelling, and through this the house will suit his soul and be beautiful for his body. For just as the Temple is \"the beauty of the world\" (Zevaḥim 54b), so the mezuza is the beauty of the house (see Zohar 3, 265b).</p>\n<p>The doorway is the place of passage between the house and the outside, and the Torah commanded that the mezuza be affixed there, so that every time he passes through it he will remember the mezuza and the foundations of faith in it. And so, when he comes into his house, he will be careful not to bring foreign ideas from outside into his house; and on the other hand, he will not shut himself up too much in his privacy, indifferent to the world outside his house. And likewise when he goes out, he will remember the mezuza and go out with awareness of his task to add goodness and blessing, and he will be careful not to be swept along after foreign ideas.<sup>1</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"The mezuza grants a person protection, but as with all the blessings in the Torah, this is protection by way of nature, through walking in the way of the Torah, and not by way of a miracle. Therefore, when it is dangerous for Jews to affix a mezuza on the gates of the city, they are exempt from mezuza (Yoma 11a). And likewise, when the mezuza may be treated with contempt, one should not affix the mezuza in a way that it is visible to the eyes of strangers (see Shakh 286:7). And see below, note 3."}]},{"slug":"21-06-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-06-03/","title_he":"ג – הברכה","title_en":"3. The Blessing","body_en":"<p>Before affixing the mezuza on the doorpost, one stands and recites the blessing: \"Blessed are You, Hashem our God, King of the universe, who has sanctified us with His commandments and commanded us to affix a mezuza\" (Shulḥan Arukh 289:1). When the householder has appointed an agent to affix the mezuza for him, the agent should recite the blessing in a general wording: \"concerning the affixing of a mezuza\" (Rambam, Berakhot 11:13). Similarly, when the house is owned by many partners, if one of them affixes the mezuza, he should say: \"to affix a mezuza\", and if another person affixes it as their agent, he should say: \"concerning the affixing of a mezuza\". And if they switched the wordings – they have fulfilled the obligation.</p>\n<p>Ideally a person is commanded to fulfil his mitzvot himself and to affix the mezuza with his own hands and not through an agent (Mishna Berura 250:3); but if his aim is to add honour to the mitzva, he may ideally ask a distinguished person to affix the mezuza for him (Tevu'ot Shor 28:14; Shiyurei Berakha 264:1).</p>\n<p>One who affixes several mezuzot one after the other recites one blessing over all of them (Rema 289:1). If the one reciting the blessing interrupted and spoke about another matter between the blessing and the affixing of the mezuza – he has lost the blessing and must recite it again. But if he interrupted after he had already affixed one mezuza, although he acted not according to halakha, since the blessing has already taken effect on the first mezuza, he should not recite it again over the mezuzot he had in mind to affix when he recited the blessing (see Mishna Berura 8:28).</p>\n<p>If he removed the mezuza in order to check it and found it valid, and immediately went back to affix it in its place, he does not recite the blessing, since he did not divert his mind from the mezuza. But if he found that it is invalid, and comes to affix another mezuza, he must recite the blessing over affixing it (Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 289:4). If he checked several mezuzot and they were all found valid, if he immediately goes back to affix each mezuza in its place, he need not recite the blessing. And if he switches the mezuzot around, since they are being affixed in a new place, he must recite the blessing.<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"Responsa Admat Kodesh (Yoreh De'ah 18) wrote that one who removes a mezuza to check it, since he is not sure that he will find it valid, should recite the blessing when he goes back to affix it. And so wrote the Yismaḥ Lev 14, Rabbi Shlomo Kluger (Sefer STaM, Mezuza 3) and Yeḥaveh Da'at (3:80). And some were in doubt about this (Leshon Limudim of Ya'avetz, Oraḥ Ḥayim 9; Ḥesed La-Alafim 14; Kol Yaakov 291:7). However, in the view of many, the law of a mezuza is like the law of a tallit: if he removed it for a short time in order to affix it again, he did not divert his mind and should not recite the blessing (above 1:6). So wrote the Da'at Kedoshim (289:4), the Maharam Schick (Yoreh De'ah 285), the Beit Yitzḥak (Yoreh De'ah 2:94), the Arukh Ha-Shulḥan (289:4), the Peri Ha-Sadeh (1:40), the Ben Ish Ḥai (second year, Ki Tavo 8), and others. But if the mezuza was found to be invalid, in the view of the overwhelming majority of the poskim he should recite the blessing over the new mezuza (unlike the Maharshag 1, Yoreh De'ah 57). And if the mezuzot were found valid, some suggest switching the mezuzot around in order to become obliged to recite the blessing (Emek Yehoshua Aḥaron, Aleh Li-Terufa 35; Kuntres Ha-Mezuza 289:5).<br>In the view of many, if the mezuza fell, when he goes back to affix it he should recite the blessing (Rabbi Shlomo Kluger, Sefer STaM, Mezuza 2; Pitḥei Teshuva 289:1; the Maharam Schick, Yoreh De'ah 285; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 289:4; Minḥat Shlomo 1:69, and others). And although if the tallit fell without intention one does not recite the blessing over wrapping oneself in it, because throughout the prayer one does not divert one's mind from it (above 1:6, note 7), here, where a person does not constantly think about a mezuza fixed in his house, if it fell he must recite the blessing over affixing it (however, Yabi'a Omer 3, Yoreh De'ah 17:11, wrote that because of the doubt he should not recite the blessing; and perhaps if several hours pass, even in his view he should recite it)."}]},{"slug":"21-06-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-06-04/","title_he":"ד – בית מזוזה","title_en":"4. The Mezuza Case","body_en":"<p>The mezuza is rolled from the end of the line to its beginning, so that when it is opened one can immediately begin to read the first word (Shulḥan Arukh 288:14). The mezuza is affixed so that the first line faces upwards, and if he erred and affixed it upside down – it is invalid. Ideally the mezuza is affixed so that the beginning of its opening faces the space of the doorway (Shulḥan Arukh 289:6).</p>\n<p>By the essential law one may affix the rolled mezuza parchment itself to the doorpost, but in practice people are accustomed to place it in a 'mezuza case', so that it will not fall, and to protect it from the dampness of the wall (Mishna, Kelim 16:7; Tosafot, Bava Metzia 102a; Shulḥan Arukh 289:1). It is good to enhance the beauty and splendour of the mezuza case (Nahar Mitzrayim, Mezuza 10). (The law of the mezuza case after its use has ended – below 14:10.)</p>\n<p>Israel are accustomed to write the Name 'Sha-ddai' on the outer side of the mezuza parchment, opposite the beginning of the passage of 'Ve-haya im shamo'a', so that when the mezuza is rolled from its end to its beginning, the Name is visible on the outside. As the reason for the custom, they explained that the Name 'Sha-ddai' expresses the protection with which God guards Israel, as its letters allude: '<i>Sh</i>omer <i>D</i>altot <i>Y</i>israel' (Guardian of the doors of Israel). Therefore it is an enhancement for the mezuza case to be transparent, so that those passing through the doorway will see the Name on the mezuza. However, many are accustomed to use a mezuza case that is not transparent, and write the Name 'Sha-ddai' on the mezuza case.</p>\n<p>When the mezuza is affixed in a bedroom in which people dress, or in a room in which babies' nappies are changed, one must make sure that the mezuza case is not transparent, and also that the Name 'Sha-ddai' is not written on the mezuza case, so that it will not be treated with disrespect. For this purpose, on many mezuza cases only 'Shin' is written, to allude to the Name 'Sha-ddai' but not to write it (Shulḥan Arukh 286:5; Mishna Berura 40:7).<sup>3</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"Israel are accustomed to write the Name 'Sha-ddai' on the outer side of the mezuza parchment, as wrote the Rambam (Mezuza 5:4), the Rosh (Laws of Mezuza 18), the Zohar (3, 266a) and the Shulḥan Arukh 288:15. According to the Shulḥan Arukh the Name 'Sha-ddai' is written opposite the letters 've-haya' of 'Ve-haya im shamo'a', and according to the Rema opposite the space between the passages. The problem is that some say that one must leave at the beginning of the parchment enough for rolling the whole mezuza (Tosafot, Menaḥot 32a, s.v. 'Ve-oseh'; the Rosh; the Semag; Shulḥan Arukh 288:1). If so, its rolling covers the Name. Indeed, in the view of many, one need not leave at the beginning of the parchment enough for its rolling, and so the Name 'Sha-ddai' is visible on the outside (the Terumah 205; the Mordekhai 969; Shevut Yaakov 2:108; Birkei Yosef 288:1, and many others). And so many of those of Ashkenazi origin act. And some are accustomed to make a hole in the parchment opposite the Name so that it will be seen (Beit Yosef 288:1; Kol Yaakov 288:3). And all this is when the mezuza is placed in a transparent case. But as mentioned, many are accustomed to use an opaque case, and write 'Sha-ddai' on the case itself.<br>The Rambam wrote (Mezuza 5:4): \"It is a widespread custom to write 'Sha-ddai' on the outside of the mezuza, opposite the space between one passage and the other, and there is no harm in this, since it is on the outside. But those who write on the inside names of angels or holy names or a verse or seals – they are among those who have no share in the World to Come. For these fools are not satisfied that they have nullified the mitzva, but they have made a great mitzva, which is the unity of the Name of the Holy One, blessed be He, and His love and His service, as though it were an amulet for their own benefit, as it entered their foolish minds, that this is a thing that benefits in the vanities of the world.\" However, if his intention is to fulfil the mitzva because God so commanded, there is nothing to prevent him from thinking that through this God will help him and protect him. And so wrote the Kesef Mishneh there, that \"the intention in making it is not to guard the house, but he must intend to fulfil the mitzva of the Holy One, blessed be He, and as a matter of course it follows that it will guard the house\". And so wrote Rabbi Yehuda Ayash (Leḥem Yehuda on the Rambam there) and the Birkei Yosef (Yoreh De'ah 288:3). Similarly the Rambam wrote (Mezuza 6:13): \"The early Sages said: whoever has tefillin on his head and his arm, tzitzit on his garment and a mezuza on his doorway is presumed not to sin, for he has many reminders, and they are the angels that save him from sinning, as it is said (Tehillim 34:8): The angel of the Lord encamps round about those who fear Him, and delivers them.\"<br>Some are accustomed to write on the back side of the mezuza, at the bottom: 'Kozu be-mokhsaz kozu', which are the letters that follow those of the Divine Names Y-H-V-H E-lohim Y-H-V-H (Hagahot Maimoniyot, Mezuza 5:3; the Semag, positive commandments 23; the Rosh, Mezuza 18; Rema 288:15, and others). And they wrote that this is not included in the Rambam's warning, since it is written on the outside. So act those of Ashkenazi origin and some of those of Sephardi origin, and some of those of Sephardi origin do not follow this practice."}]},{"slug":"21-06-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-06-05/","title_he":"ה – קביעת המזוזה","title_en":"5. Affixing the Mezuza","body_en":"<p>One must affix the mezuza to the doorpost in a stable way, with nails, screws or strong glue. When necessary one may affix it with strong adhesive tape. But one who affixes the mezuza in a way that it can easily fall, or affixed it only at its top, so that it moves and sways – it is invalid. Likewise, if he did not affix the mezuza to the doorpost, for example he attached the mezuza to a stick and placed the stick in the place of the mezuza, or he made a narrow shelf the width of the mezuza and placed the mezuza on it – it is invalid. This is because the mezuza must be permanently attached to the doorpost, as it is said (Devarim 6:9): \"on the doorposts of your house and on your gates\" – 'on your gates', that is, that it be attached to the gate.<sup>4</sup></p>\n<p>In the view of most of the Rishonim the mezuza must be affixed upright, and if he affixed it lying down it is invalid. And so many of the communities of the East are accustomed to affix it upright. However, those of Ashkenazi and Moroccan origin and many of those of Sephardi origin are accustomed to affix it diagonally, because according to Rabbeinu Tam it must be affixed lying down, and if he affixed it upright it is invalid. And so that it will not be considered invalid according to any view, people are accustomed to affix it diagonally. And they are accustomed to affix it so that its top leans towards the entrance. But when it is not possible to affix it diagonally, it should be affixed upright, following the view of most of the Rishonim (Shulḥan Arukh and Rema 289:6).<sup>5</sup></p>\n<p>A man or a woman who has reached the age of mitzvot may affix the mezuza.<sup>6</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"Menaḥot (32b): \"If he hung it on a stick – it is invalid; what is the reason? We require 'on your gates'.\" And the Rambam wrote (Mezuza 5:8) that this is because \"this is not affixing\". And so wrote the Baḥ (289:9) and the Maharsham (4:139). And in the Shulḥan Arukh (289:4) he wrote that he should affix it with nails, and some were precise that two nails are needed – above and below, for if he fixes one nail at its top it will swing at the bottom (Ḥayei Adam 15:19). In practice, the main thing is that it be fixed and not move, and there is no difference between nails and glue (Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 289:15; Yaskil Avdi 8, Yoreh De'ah 14:2). However, one should not affix it with weak sellotape. And it appears that if the mezuza can certainly last thirty days, it can already be considered fixed. If he fixed the mezuza case well, but because of its structure it is not flush against the doorpost, the mezuza is valid."},{"n":5,"en":"According to Rashi, the Rid, the Riaz, the Mordekhai and the Nimukei Yosef, the mezuza must be upright, just as the Torah scroll is placed in the Holy Ark in a respectful manner, and if he affixed it lying down – it is invalid; and this is what the Sages meant (Menaḥot 33a): \"If he made it like a bolt – it is invalid.\" And so is the view of the Rambam (Mezuza 5:8) as explained by the Beit Yosef. On the other hand, according to Rabbeinu Tam (Tosafot, Menaḥot 33a, s.v. 'Ha'), it is not respectful to place it standing, and so in the Ark in the Temple they placed the Tablets and the Torah scroll lying down. And if he affixed it upright, it is 'like a bolt' and invalid. Tosafot (there) and the Rosh (Mezuza 9) added that if the mezuza was affixed diagonally – it is valid according to both Rashi and Rabbeinu Tam.<br>In practice, the Mordekhai and the Semag (positive commandments 23) wrote that the custom is to affix it upright. And so wrote the Shulḥan Arukh (289:6) and the Gra 14. And so ruled many of the poskim of those of Sephardi origin, among them: the Ben Ish Ḥai (second year, Ki Tavo 7) and Halikhot Olam (8, Ki Tavo 16). However, many wrote that since when it is diagonal it is valid according to all the views, it is good to affix it diagonally (the Semak 154, the Kol Bo, the Maharil). And as long as it is somewhat diagonal, since it is not upright, it is valid also according to Rabbeinu Tam's view. And even when it is fully diagonal, it is still valid according to Rashi and most of the Rishonim (Rabbeinu Yeruḥam; Da'at Kedoshim 289:18). That this is the practice was written by Rabbeinu Yeruḥam (Toledot Adam Ve-Ḥava 21:7), the Terumat Ha-Deshen (52), the Rema (289:6), the Baḥ, the Levush, the Perisha, the Ḥayei Adam and others. When it is not possible to affix it diagonally, it is affixed upright (Yad Ha-Ketana; Pitḥei Teshuva 289:9).<br>Those who are accustomed to affix it diagonally: all the communities of those of Ashkenazi origin and of Moroccan origin (Minḥa Belula of Asbag 60:7; Zokher Berit Avot, Mezuza 1). And so was the custom in Algeria and among the Musta'arabim in Aleppo (Torat Imekha 32:11), and in Libya (Ma'aseh Roke'aḥ, Laws of Tefillin, Mezuza and Sefer Torah 5:8). And so the Sephardim in Jerusalem were accustomed (Yaskil Avdi 8, Oraḥ Ḥayim 17:7; Netivei Am, Yoreh De'ah 289:6). On the other hand, in Iraq they were accustomed to affix it upright; and likewise in Yemen (the Maharitz). And so they were accustomed in Salonika, except for meticulous people who enhanced by affixing it diagonally (Shulḥan Gavoha 289:18). And likewise in Tunis they were accustomed to affix it upright (Alei Hadas 4:25), except for those who enhance, who affixed it diagonally (Ish Matzli'aḥ 1, Yoreh De'ah 34)."},{"n":6,"en":"However, the Yeshu'ot Malko (Mezuza 5:10) wrote that a woman should not affix a mezuza, since she is not fit to write it. However, in the view of the overwhelming majority of the poskim a woman is fit to affix it, since she is obligated in mezuza, and the essence of the mitzva is affixing the mezuza (Responsa Ḥatam Sofer, Yoreh De'ah 271; Eretz Tzvi 1:15; Sedei Ḥemed, Ma'arekhet Mem 131; Kuntres Ha-Mezuza 291:21, and others).<br>Some say that if a minor affixed a mezuza in his room, it is valid (Naḥalat Tzvi of Eisenstadt 291:2; Responsa Torah Li-Shma 307). And many invalidate it (Ḥikrei Lev 3:128; Mikdash Me'at 289:2, and others)."}]},{"slug":"21-06-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-06-06/","title_he":"ו – גובה המזוזה","title_en":"6. The Height of the Mezuza","body_en":"<p>The height of the mezuza is in the upper third of the doorway, except for the top tefaḥ. It is preferable to affix the mezuza at the beginning of the upper third. That is, one measures the height of the doorway and divides it into three parts. In a doorway 2.10 metres high, each third will be 70 centimetres, and if so the mezuza should be affixed so that all of it is above 140 cm from the floor.</p>\n<p>One must be precise in this measurement, since in the view of many, if part of the mezuza is at a height of less than two thirds of the doorway – the mezuza is invalid. In a case where an exact measurement was not made, it is preferable to escape the doubt and affix the mezuza a little higher, because it is better to forgo the enhancement of affixing it at the beginning of the upper third than to enter into a doubt that perhaps it is invalid (Shulḥan Arukh, Yoreh De'ah 289:2; the Taz and the Nekudot Ha-Kesef).</p>\n<p>The simple reason for this is that when the mezuza is in the upper third, those passing through the doorway see it and are reminded of faith. One can further say that a person too has three parts: the upper part of a person is the intellect, the second part is the heart and emotion, and the third part is action. Affixing the mezuza in the upper third symbolises fixing the ideas of the mezuza in the upper part of a person, in his thought. And the enhancement of affixing it at the bottom of the upper third expresses the influence that the ideas should have on emotion and action.<sup>7</sup></p>\n<p>When the doorway is very high, and the upper third is above a person's height, the mezuza should be affixed below the upper third, for the main thing is that the mezuza be at a height visible to the eyes of one entering. Therefore, in a doorway up to 2.70 metres high, he should affix the mezuza at the beginning of the upper third, for in this way the mezuza is below two metres and still visible to the eyes of those entering. But in a higher doorway, they should affix it at shoulder height.<sup>8</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":7,"en":"Menaḥot (33a): \"Rabbi Zeira said in the name of Rav Matna in the name of Shmuel: It is a mitzva to place it at the beginning of the upper third. And Rav Huna said: he raises it a tefaḥ from the ground and distances it a tefaḥ from the lintel, and the whole doorway is valid for the mezuza.\" And the halakha follows Shmuel, because Rav Huna was his student (Hagahot Maimoniyot, Mezuza 6:5).<br>In the view of many, the place of the mezuza is in the upper third except for the top tefaḥ, and it is a mitzva to affix it at the beginning of the upper third. So wrote the Rambam (Mezuza 6:12), the Nimukei Yosef and the Kol Bo, the Shulḥan Arukh (289:2), Derekh Ha-Ḥayim (the place of affixing the mezuza 2), Yad Ha-Ketana (3:11), the Ben Ish Ḥai (second year, Ki Tavo 7), Kitzur Shulḥan Arukh (11:5), Gidulei Ha-Kodesh (289:5), the Arukh Ha-Shulḥan (10), the Maharsham (3:219), and others. And some say that even in the top tefaḥ it is valid, since the halakha does not follow Rav Huna (the Rif; the Semag, positive commandment 23; the Rosh there), and therefore if he affixed it in the top tefaḥ he should affix it again below this tefaḥ without a blessing. And some hold that there is no preference for the beginning of the upper third (the Rosh, Mezuza 10; the Yere'im 400; the Riaz; Rabbeinu Yeruḥam). And the Nekudot Ha-Kesef (289:2) strengthened this view and wrote that this is the general custom. And so wrote the Teshuva Me-Ahava (3, notes to Yoreh De'ah, p. 64) and the Yad Sha'ul (289:4). Therefore I have called affixing it at the beginning of the upper third an 'enhancement'.<br>Below the upper third: in the view of most of the poskim it is invalid (the Baḥ 4; the Taz 3; the Shakh 4; the Maharsham 3:219). However, some hold that only for the mitzva must it be affixed in the upper third, but after the fact, even below the upper third it is valid (Rabbeinu Manoaḥ and the Tur). Therefore, if he affixed it below the upper third, he should affix it anew without a blessing (Ben Ish Ḥai, second year, Ki Tavo 8).<br>If only part of the mezuza went down below the upper third, according to Responsa Tiferet Ziv (1, Oraḥ Ḥayim 2) – it is valid, for one goes by the majority. And according to Responsa Teshuvot Le-Sho'el 15 it is invalid, and so wrote several of the Aḥaronim of our times (Yabi'a Omer 2, Yoreh De'ah 21; Kuntres Ha-Mezuza 289:18, and others)."},{"n":8,"en":"In the Jerusalem Talmud, Megilla (4:12): if the doorway is high, he affixes the mezuza opposite his shoulders. Some say that the Babylonian Talmud disagrees with the Jerusalem Talmud on this (Tosafot, Menaḥot 33b; the Eshkol 25; and so it is implied by the Rif, the Rambam and the Rosh, who omitted the Jerusalem Talmud), and some say accordingly that the mezuza must always be in the upper third of the doorway (the Maharsham 3:219; Ha'amek She'ela 145:3; Divrei Malkiel 3:79; Yabi'a Omer 2, Yoreh De'ah 21). However, the Mordekhai (961) wrote that the Ri was in doubt whether the Babylonian Talmud disagrees with the Jerusalem Talmud on this, and therefore when the doorway is high he should affix the mezuza so that it is visible to one entering, even though it will be below the upper third. And so wrote the Levush 2, the Taz (289:3), the Shakh 4, the Ḥayei Adam (15:17), Derekh Ha-Ḥayim and the Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 10. And so wrote most of the Aḥaronim. And so it appears in practice, that the main point is that the mezuza be visible to those entering.<br>Derekh Ha-Ḥayim (the place of affixing the mezuza 2) wrote that whenever the upper third is above the height of his shoulders, this is the 'high doorway' of which the Jerusalem Talmud spoke; and the Avnei Nezer (Yoreh De'ah 379) wrote that this applies only if it is much higher than his shoulders. And I have written above according to the proposal of Kuntres Ha-Mezuza (289:20), that up to a height of 2.70 metres he should affix the mezuza at the beginning of the upper third. And some are meticulous, in order to escape the doubt, to make a grille at a height of about two and a half metres, and thereby turn the doorway into a doorway of ordinary height (Rav Mordekhai Eliyahu)."}]},{"slug":"21-06-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-06-07/","title_he":"ז – בצד ימין","title_en":"7. On the Right Side","body_en":"<p>The place of the mezuza is on the right side of one entering the house or the room, for the Sages expounded (Devarim 6:9): \"on the doorposts of your house\" – \"the way of your entering, from the right\", for a person's way is to lift his right foot first (Menaḥot 34a). If he erred and affixed it on the left side – it is invalid (Rambam; Shulḥan Arukh 289:2), and he should affix it again on the right side with a blessing (Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 5). And even a left-handed person, who is accustomed to lift his left foot first, must affix the mezuza on the right of the entrance, for since the mezuza serves many people, one goes by the majority of people (Rema there; Shakh 5).</p>\n<p>As is the law of the doorway of the house, so is the law of the inner rooms: in every room the mezuza must be affixed on the right side of one entering it. Sometimes a doubt arises as to which is the side of entry, for example in a doorway between two rooms each of which has another doorway to the street or to the living room of the house. In such a situation, if it is clear that usually one enters the one room first and from it the second, then the mezuza must be affixed on the right side of those entering from the first room into the second. And if the matter is in doubt, one goes by the side on which there is the 'hinge indicator', that is, the room into which the door opens is considered the inner one, since the practice is that the door of a house or a room opens inwards, and the mezuza is affixed on the right side of the entrance into it (Shulḥan Arukh 289:3). Admittedly, in 'emergency exits' the door opens outwards, but since the direction of entry is clear, the mezuza is affixed on the right of the entrance without taking the 'hinge indicator' into account.</p>\n<p><i>[Diagram: identifying the side for the mezuza]</i></p>\n<p>When it is not possible to decide by the direction of entry or the 'hinge indicator', for example the door opens to both its sides, since both directions of the doorway are a way of entry, both sides are valid for the mezuza, and the householder may decide on which side to affix the mezuza.<sup>9</sup></p>\n<p>Some say that the law of an enclosed courtyard or an enclosed balcony is also like that of an inner room, and therefore the mezuza should be affixed on the right side of one going out to the courtyard or the balcony (the Maharil, the Beit Yosef, the Taz). And some say that the mezuza should be affixed on the right side of one entering the house, because entry is always from outside the house into the house, and therefore the doorway is called an entrance to the house, and not an entrance to the courtyard or the balcony (the Beit Meir; the Yeshu'ot Malko; Rav Y. E. Henkin). In practice, since each view has its reasoning, the householder may decide on which side to affix the mezuza. And if the courtyard or the balcony is not enclosed, but there is a doorway from the street to the courtyard or the balcony, even if it is used only occasionally, the doorway between them and the house is called an entrance to the house, and the mezuza must be affixed on the right of the entrance to the house.<sup>10</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"Menaḥot (34a): \"'Your house' – the way of your entering, from the right, for when a person lifts his foot, he lifts the right one first\" (when a person begins to walk, he begins with the right foot). The Rambam wrote (Mezuza 6:12): \"And if he affixed it on the left it is invalid.\" And so wrote the Tur and the Shulḥan Arukh (289:2). When it is not clear what the way of entry to the doorway is, for example there are two rooms, each with an entrance from the street or from another room, and there is a doorway between them, the Sages said (Menaḥot 33a): \"Go by the hinge indicator.\" That is, the room into which the door opens is the side into which one enters (Piskei Rid there). Some say that in every doorway between two rooms one goes by the hinge indicator (Zera Emet, Yoreh De'ah 134; Da'at Kedoshim 289:11). However, according to almost all the poskim, the direction of entry is the main thing, and only when there is a doubt about the direction does one go by the hinge indicator. And so wrote the Yosef Ometz 29, the Pitḥei Teshuva (289:4), She'elat Ya'avetz (1:70), the Sho'el U-Meshiv (third edition 2:166), the Arukh Ha-Shulḥan (289:8), and many others. If one does not know what the way of entry is and there is also no hinge indicator, since the way of entry is from both directions, the householder may decide on which side he will affix the mezuza. For so emerges from the Jerusalem Talmud (Megilla 4:12), according to the Korban Ha-Eda. And so wrote Rabbi Shlomo Kluger (STaM, Mezuza 7), the Mikdash Me'at (289:13) and the Minḥat Pittim (Yoreh De'ah 23) (and according to the Shulḥan Gavoha 289:11, even when it is clear which is the right, after the fact on the left it is valid). If one of the two rooms is more important than the other, there is room for him to decide that the entrance is into the important room, for several poskim wrote that the way of entry is into the important room (Ḥesed Le-Avraham of Alkalai, Yoreh De'ah 16; Ḥayei Adam 15:18; Derekh Ha-Ḥayim; Kitzur Shulḥan Arukh 11:4). With a sliding door, it appears that the 'hinge indicator' is the side on which it is located, and if it is inside the thickness of the wall, then it has no 'hinge indicator'.<br>The Teshuva Me-Ahava (1:61) and Kuntres Ha-Mezuza (289:25) ruled that the important room is more decisive than the hinge indicator, and they created a complex hierarchy that is hard to apply and also contrary to most of the poskim, as explained above. And some hold that the way of entry is always determined towards the important room, even when in practice it is contrary to the direction of entry (Ish Matzli'aḥ 1, Yoreh De'ah 35; Shevut Yitzḥak 1:3 in the name of Rav Elyashiv). And their view is contrary to the words of almost all the poskim and to the course of the sugya."},{"n":10,"en":"Responsa Maharil 94 wrote that the law of an enclosed courtyard is like that of an inner room, and the mezuza is affixed on the right of those entering it, for this is the way of entry, from the house to the courtyard. And his words were brought as halakha by the Beit Yosef (289:3) and the Taz 4. And so is the law for a balcony. And so wrote the Ḥikrei Lev (3:129), the Sho'el U-Meshiv (third edition 2:166), Rabbi Shlomo Kluger (STaM, Mezuza 4), Gidulei Ha-Kodesh (289:8), the Minḥat Pittim (Yoreh De'ah 23), the Misgeret Ha-Shulḥan (11:2), Ḥavalim Ba-Ne'imim (2, Yoreh De'ah 67), Igrot Moshe (Yoreh De'ah 1:181), and others.<br>On the other hand, some hold that entry is always from outside the house into the house, and therefore one affixes it on the right of one entering the house; and so people say: \"Let us go out to the courtyard\", and not: \"Let us go into the courtyard\". So wrote the Beit Meir (289:4), the Yeshu'ot Malko, Mezuza (6:1), Rav Henkin (Kitvei Ha-Grya Henkin 1:167) and the Shevet Ha-Levi (3:150). In addition, there is room to say that the enclosed courtyards of the past were intended for household uses such as cooking, and therefore were considered like a room, but today they are not like a room, and therefore even those who follow the Maharil's view may perhaps agree to this (see Ḥazon Ish, Yoreh De'ah 168:5). In practice, since there are views on both sides and it is not possible to decide, this is a situation of doubt, and then, as we learned in the previous note, both sides are valid, and the householder will decide where to affix the mezuza. A small balcony that does not have an area of sixteen square amot is exempt from mezuza according to most poskim (who did not agree with the Ḥamudei Daniel, below 7:7), and if he wants to be stringent, it is perhaps preferable that he affix it on the right side of the entrance from the balcony into the house. I heard from my father and teacher, who heard from Rav Ra'anan, that he visited Rav Herzog and saw that he had affixed a mezuza on the right of the entrance to the balcony, and he told him that according to our master Rav Kook, of blessed memory, one affixes it on the right of the entrance to the house. Rav Herzog said: \"One does not respond after the lion\", and moved the mezuza to the right of the entrance to the house. From this it emerges that Rav Herzog held that one fulfils the obligation on both sides, only he had acted like many, and when he heard Rav Kook's view, he changed out of respect for him.<br><b>When there is a doorway from the courtyard to the street</b>, but only occasionally do people enter through it into the courtyard and the house: some say that one goes by the hinge indicator (the view of the poskim who follow the Maharil regarding an enclosed courtyard, as explained by Rabbi Shlomo Kluger, STaM, Mezuza 4; the Sho'el U-Meshiv, third edition 2:166). And many hold that its law is like that of other doorways of houses, that one must affix it on the right of the entrance to the house (Ḥavalim Ba-Ne'imim 2, Yoreh De'ah 67; Tzur Yaakov 104; Igrot Moshe 1:181). And this is in addition to those who hold that even in an enclosed courtyard one affixes it on the right of one entering the house. In fact, even according to the first view, this is how people act in practice, for the hinge indicator is towards the inside, and if not, it is for reasons of safety."}]},{"slug":"21-06-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-06-08/","title_he":"ח – בחלל הפתח","title_en":"8. Within the Space of the Doorway","body_en":"<p>One must affix the mezuza within the space of the doorway. When the thickness of the doorway is wide, the mezuza should be affixed in the tefaḥ (7.6 cm) nearest the outside, so that the mezuza will include the whole doorway and the house, and so that immediately upon entering he will encounter the mezuza (Menaḥot 33b; Shulḥan Arukh 289:2). But this is not indispensable, and as long as he affixed the mezuza within the space of the doorway – it is valid (Rema there).</p>\n<p>In the past, some were accustomed to make a recess in the doorpost for the mezuza, and covered the mezuza with glass or plaster. And they were careful not to make the recess deeper than a tefaḥ, for the mezuza must be in the doorway, and at a depth of more than a tefaḥ it is no longer in the doorway and is invalid (Menaḥot there; Shulḥan Arukh there, 4). When they covered the mezuza with plaster, they were careful to make a mark there so that everyone would know where it is. And if it is not discernible that there is a mezuza there, one does not fulfil the obligation with it (Yad Ha-Ketana 3:25; Pitḥei Teshuva 289:5).</p>\n<p>If he affixed the mezuza not within the space of the doorway, for example he affixed it on the outer wall in the tefaḥ adjacent to the doorway – in the view of many one does not fulfil the obligation with it. The question is what to do when it is not possible to affix the mezuza within the space of the doorway, for example the wall is made of glass or thin metal. The solution is to thicken the space of the doorway by installing a frame around the doorway, so that the mezuza will be within the space of the doorway. Another solution is to cut a place for the mezuza in the side of the wall adjacent to the doorway. After the fact, when it is not possible to do this, the mezuza should be affixed on the outer wall adjacent to the doorway on the right, but since some hold that one does not fulfil the obligation with this mezuza, one should not recite the blessing over affixing it.<sup>11</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":11,"en":"Menaḥot (33b): \"Rava said: It is a mitzva to place it in the tefaḥ adjacent to the public domain. What is the reason? The Rabbis say: so that he will encounter the mezuza immediately; Rav Ḥanina of Sura says: so that it will guard him.\" But one should not affix the mezuza outside the space of the doorway, even if it is adjacent to the doorway, as they said in Menaḥot (32b): \"If he placed it behind the door (on the inside) – it is a danger, and there is no mitzva in it\"; a danger – because in this way the mezuza does not protect the house. And one should not place it outside the space towards the outside either, for \"Rav Yehuda said in the name of Shmuel: It is a mitzva to place it within the space of the doorway.\" And so it was ruled in the Shulḥan Arukh (289:2).<br>After the fact, if he affixed the mezuza outside the space of the doorway, the Shakh 289:3 wrote that according to the Tur and the Rema, as long as it is in the tefaḥ adjacent to the doorway it is valid after the fact. And according to the Nimukei Yosef, the Rambam (Mezuza 5:8), the Beit Yosef and the Levush – it is invalid. And one should be stringent like them. And he added in the name of the Divrei Ḥamudot that when there is danger because of the gentiles in affixing the mezuza outside the door, and inside the door there is no room within the space of the doorway, they may rely on the Tur and the Rema and affix it adjacent to the doorway on the inside. Thus far. This law is relevant today to thin walls of glass or metal, where, having no choice, they should affix the mezuza without a blessing adjacent to the doorway.<br>Admittedly, some hold that even according to the Rema, after the fact, if he affixed it outside the space of the doorway – it is invalid (Shulḥan Gavoha 7), but many explained the Rema like the Shakh, and in a pressing situation one may rely on them. And so wrote the Yad Ha-Ketana (Laws of Mezuza 3, Minḥat Ani 20). And according to the Arukh Ha-Shulḥan (289:13-14), even the Rambam and the Nimukei Yosef agree that this is not indispensable in a pressing situation. And according to the Nishmat Adam (15:1), if he affixes the mezuza on the outer side of the frame around the doorway, in the tefaḥ adjacent to the space of the doorway, according to most poskim it is valid and according to the Rambam it is invalid, but if he affixed it on the wall it is invalid according to all views. And so wrote the Mikdash Me'at (289:9; Gidulei Ha-Kodesh 4), that in a pressing situation one may affix it on the frame of the doorway that is not within the space of the doorway. And so wrote the Kitzur Shulḥan Arukh (11:9) and the Shevet Ha-Levi (3:111). However, they wrote that because of the doubt he should affix it without a blessing."}]},{"slug":"21-06-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-06-09/","title_he":"ט – זמן קביעת המזוזה ומגורים בלא מזוזה","title_en":"9. When to Affix the Mezuza, and Living Without a Mezuza","body_en":"<p>One who buys or rents an apartment becomes obligated in mezuza when he begins to live in it. However, if he wishes, already from the time he begins to bring his belongings into it he may affix mezuzot in it with a blessing.<sup>12</sup></p>\n<p>Some say that it is rabbinically forbidden to live in a house that has no mezuza, and therefore if the mezuza fell on Shabbat, or it turned out to be invalid, it is forbidden to live in the house or the room until a valid mezuza is affixed in it. Only in a situation of compulsion, where he has no other place to live, may he live in the house without a mezuza (Ben Ish Ḥai). And in the view of most of the poskim, the mitzva is to affix a mezuza, and one who delays affixing it neglects a positive commandment, but there is no prohibition against living in a room or a house without mezuzot.</p>\n<p>In practice, if a person has no possibility of affixing mezuzot in his house or his room, for example the mezuzot fell on Shabbat and until the end of Shabbat it is forbidden to affix them, or they were found to be invalid and he has no possibility of obtaining valid mezuzot in the coming days, since at present he cannot fulfil the mitzva, he is not considered as one who neglects a positive commandment, and he may live in the house or sleep in his room until he obtains valid mezuzot. However, if he can obtain mezuzot and is negligent about it, he should not live in a house or a room that has no mezuza, so as not to transgress 'neglecting a positive commandment'.<sup>13</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":12,"en":"Some say that from the moment the apartment is ready to be lived in, the owner of the apartment or the one renting it is obligated to affix a mezuza in it (Ḥemed Moshe, Oraḥ Ḥayim 19:1; Avnei Nezer, Yoreh De'ah 381). And according to most of the poskim, the obligation of mezuza applies only when the person begins actually to live in the apartment (Magen Avraham, Oraḥ Ḥayim 19:1; Da'at Kedoshim 289:2; Mishna Berura 19:4; Shevet Ha-Levi 6:161). There is one who explains that the intention is from the time he begins to bring his belongings into the apartment, for then it begins to serve as a storeroom for his furniture and becomes obligated in mezuza (Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 19:2). However, according to most of the poskim, only when he actually lives in it is he obligated, only that if he wishes he may affix a mezuza with a blessing from the time he brings in his belongings (Harei Besamim 2:219; Devar Yehoshua 3, Yoreh De'ah 51)."},{"n":13,"en":"Some say that it is rabbinically forbidden to live in a house without a mezuza, and only in a situation of compulsion, where there is no other place to live, did the Sages not decree (Pitḥei Teshuva 285:1, in the name of the Peri Megadim, Oraḥ Ḥayim 38, Eshel Avraham 15; Ben Ish Ḥai, second year, Ki Tavo 2). And the view of most poskim is that one who can affix a mezuza in his house and was negligent about it neglects a positive commandment (the Mordekhai, Menaḥot 944; Magen Avraham 13:8), but there is no prohibition against living in a house without a mezuza (the Maharaḥ Or Zarua 61; the Maharsham 8:219; Minḥat Pittim 13, and others). And I have taken the middle path: when there is no possibility of affixing a mezuza, since he is not neglecting a positive commandment, he may ideally remain in his house. But if he can obtain a mezuza and is negligent about it, he should not live in the house or sleep in the room until he affixes a mezuza, so as not to transgress 'neglecting a positive commandment'."}]},{"slug":"21-06-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-06-10/","title_he":"י – בדיקת המזוזה","title_en":"10. Checking the Mezuza","body_en":"<p>The Sages instructed that every person should check his mezuzot twice in seven years, and the mezuzot on the gates of the city and in public buildings should be checked twice in fifty years, for the public should not be burdened beyond what is necessary (Yoma 11a, Rashi; Shulḥan Arukh 291:1).</p>\n<p>There are two kinds of checking: (a) that of an expert proofreader, whose purpose is to check letter by letter, to make sure that they were written according to halakha. This thorough check must be carried out before the mezuza is affixed for the first time (see below 10:21). (b) The check that the Sages instructed to carry out twice in seven years, which any person can do (Ḥatam Sofer, Yoreh De'ah 283; Pitḥei Teshuva 291:3). Its purpose is to check whether, over the time that has passed, the ink has faded or crumbled and flaked off. And so, twice in seven years, he should take down the mezuza and check that all the letters have remained whole and have not cracked, especially at the place where the parchment is folded. If no problem is found, he should return it to its place (and regarding the blessing see above halakha 3). And if a doubt arises, he should ask a Torah scholar. And if a doubt arises for him that perhaps his mezuzot did not originally undergo proper proofreading, it is right that a qualified proofreader check the mezuzot.</p>\n<p>In recent generations, after the invention of plastic, the mezuza can be wrapped in plastic and thereby sealed from moisture, and experience shows that in this way the mezuza is preserved for decades without a flaw. Therefore, when the mezuza has been wrapped in plastic there is no need to check the mezuza; only if it was affixed in a place exposed to great heat, or something happened that may invalidate it, such as a fire, must it be checked (similar to tefillin, above 4:15).<sup>14</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":14,"en":"A baraita in Yoma (11a): \"A private mezuza is checked twice in a shavu'a (seven years), and a public one twice in a jubilee.\" Rashi explains that one must check lest it \"has rotted or been stolen\". And so the Rishonim and the Aḥaronim ruled that one must check the mezuzot twice in seven years, and so wrote the Shulḥan Arukh (Yoreh De'ah 291:1). This includes all the mezuzot in the house, and one should not rely on checking a sample (Birkei Yosef 1). However, many in practice were accustomed not to check, relying on Rashi that the reason for the check is to make sure it was not stolen, and therefore there is no need to open it but only to see that it is there (see Shiyurei Berakha 1, in the name of the Radak). And Rabbi Yosef Molkho (Shulḥan Gavoha 1) wrote about three hundred years ago that one need not check the mezuzot twice in seven years, because they are placed in a tube that protects them from the dampness of the wall. However, the Rambam and the Shulḥan Arukh too wrote that the mezuza is placed in a tube, and nevertheless they wrote that it must be checked, so not every tube protects. The Mateh Efrayim (581:10) wrote that there are men of deeds who check their tefillin and mezuzot every month of Elul. And some were accustomed to check mezuzot after a misfortune (see Responsa Maharil 94; Shivḥei Ha-Ari, chapter 7). And the Arukh Ha-Shulḥan (291:1) wrote that in a place prone to rot, as in his country where moisture is common, one should check at least once a year, and so wrote Rav Ḥayim David Ha-Levi (Mayim Ḥayim 1:52). However, the invention of plastic and the ability to seal the mezuza with it have changed the reality, so that a mezuza that has been properly wrapped and sealed in plastic does not become invalid. And so it is right to rule, that if the mezuza has been wrapped and sealed in plastic, and was not placed in a place where it receives strong heat from the sun or from an oven, or moisture, it need not be checked. Similarly is the view of Rabbi Shlomo Zalman Auerbach (Halikhot Shlomo, Prayer 4, note 52) and Rabbi Yosef Shalom Elyashiv (Va-Yishma Moshe 1, p. 318)."}]},{"slug":"21-06-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-06-11/","title_he":"יא – עקירת מזוזות בעת מעבר דירה","title_en":"11. Removing Mezuzot When Moving House","body_en":"<p>One who sells his house, although he takes all his belongings from his house, is forbidden to remove the mezuzot. And likewise one who rents a house is obligated to affix mezuzot in it, and at the end of his tenancy, when he leaves, he is forbidden to take the mezuzot. This is because removing the mezuzot harms the house, which is left without the protection of the mezuza and its sanctity. The Sages said: It once happened that someone took the mezuza and left the house, and he buried his wife and two sons (Bava Metzia 102a; Shulḥan Arukh, Yoreh De'ah 291:2).</p>\n<p>One who rents a house from a gentile too must affix mezuzot in it, but when he leaves he should remove the mezuzot. And likewise a Jew who rents a house to a gentile, before the gentile enters his house – should remove the mezuzot, lest they be treated with contempt by him (Shulḥan Arukh there; below 7:14). And likewise one whose house is about to collapse, before he demolishes it should remove the mezuzot.</p>\n<p>A person who acquired costly mezuzot, written with enhancement by an expert scribe, and wishes to take the mezuzot with him – a few days before leaving the house should replace the enhanced mezuzot with plain mezuzot, and take the enhanced ones for himself.</p>\n<p>Although one leaving the house is forbidden to take the mezuzot, he has the right to demand payment for them from the new tenant who comes after him. But even if the new tenant is not willing to pay for them, the one leaving is forbidden to take the mezuzot (Rema 291:2; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 3; and see Birkei Yosef 4).<sup>16</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":16,"en":"Bava Metzia (102a): \"The Rabbis taught: One who rents a house to his fellow – it is incumbent on the tenant to make a mezuza for it, and when he leaves – he should not take it in his hand and leave. And from a gentile – he takes it in his hand and leaves.\" The Sages further said there: \"It once happened that someone took it in his hand and left, and he buried his wife and two sons.\" The reason for the prohibition against removing the mezuza is so as not to give harmful forces a foothold (Tosafot, Tosafot Ha-Rosh, Levush 291:2). And some explain that since sanctity from the mezuza has already taken effect in the house – one should not nullify it (the Ritva and the Nimukei Yosef, Bava Metzia there). According to this, even when a person has no mezuzot for his new house, he should not take the mezuzot from the house he is leaving. However, in the view of Rav Aḥai Gaon and Rav Hai Gaon, the prohibition is because of contempt for the mezuza, but if he takes it and affixes it in another place, there is no contempt for it. Likewise, if the new tenant is not willing to pay for the mezuza, one may take it (brought in the work attributed to the Ritva, Bava Metzia 102a; the Eshkol). In practice it was ruled that one should not remove the mezuzot for his new house (Beit Leḥem Yehuda 291:1; Peri Megadim, Oraḥ Ḥayim 15, Mishbetzot Zahav 2; Kuntres Ha-Mezuza 7). But in a pressing situation, when he has no mezuzot for his new house and it is very hard for him to obtain them, the Aḥaronim wrote that one may rely on the view of the Geonim to take the mezuzot from the old house and move them to the new one (Birkei Yosef 3; Pitḥei Teshuva 7).<br>According to the Peri Megadim (15, Mishbetzot Zahav 2), the prohibition against taking the mezuza applies only when Jews are about to move in there, but if the house is going to remain empty – it is permitted. On the other hand, according to the Meshiv Davar (2:82), even if the house will stand empty for a period, it is forbidden to take down its mezuzot, because it is intended for other tenants to live there. Similarly wrote Responsa Ya'avetz (2:119). And so is the halakha, and only from a house about to be demolished should the mezuzot be removed.<br>The Birkei Yosef wrote (291:5), and it was brought in the Pitḥei Teshuva 7, that even if the one entering is willing to affix his own mezuzot after the one leaving removes his, one should not draw a distinction and permit removing the mezuzot. However, it is agreed that another tenant who moves in may remove his fellow's mezuzot and affix his own. And of course he should return to his fellow the mezuzot he removed (Mor U-Ketzi'a 15).<br>If the one leaving cares about the enhanced mezuzot he affixed in the house, in the view of many he may take the enhanced ones and affix ordinary ones in their place. Admittedly, some forbade exchanging the mezuzot because this is a ruse, and danger is more severe than prohibition (Yad Ha-Levi 1:185). To avoid this, some say that it is better to remove the mezuzot for the sake of checking and afterwards affix other mezuzot in their place (Da'at Kedoshim 291:2; Levushei Mordekhai 4:140). And some suggested doing this a few days before moving house (Yabi'a Omer 3, Yoreh De'ah 18)."}]}]});
